---
import GuideLayout from "@/layouts/GuideLayout.astro";
import { GUIDES_BY_SLUG } from "@/data/guides";
const g = GUIDES_BY_SLUG["overnight-shift-hours"]!;
---

<GuideLayout title={g.title} metaTitle={g.metaTitle} description={g.description} slug={g.slug} updated={g.updated}>
  <p>
    To calculate an overnight shift, <strong>add 24 hours to the end time</strong>
    before subtracting the start time, then subtract any unpaid break. A
    shift from 10:00 PM to 6:30 AM with a 30-minute unpaid break is
    (6:30 + 24:00) − 22:00 − 0:30 = <strong>8 hours</strong>. The
    arithmetic is the easy part. The harder questions are which day and which
    week the hours belong to, and what happens on the two nights a year when
    the clocks change.
  </p>

  <h2>The basic calculation</h2>
  <p>Work in the 24-hour clock so you can't mix up AM and PM:</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Convert both times to 24-hour format: 10:00 PM is 22:00, 6:30 AM is 06:30.</li>
    <li>If the end time is earlier than the start time, the shift crossed midnight, so add 24 hours to the end: 06:30 becomes 30:30.</li>
    <li>Subtract: 30:30 − 22:00 = 8 hours 30 minutes.</li>
    <li>Subtract unpaid breaks: 8:30 − 0:30 = 8:00.</li>
    <li>For payroll, convert minutes to decimal hours by dividing by 60 (see <a href="/guides/convert-minutes-to-decimal-hours/">minutes to decimal hours</a>).</li>
  </ol>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Shift</th><th>Unpaid break</th><th>Hours worked</th><th>Decimal</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>10:00 PM – 6:30 AM</td><td>30 min</td><td>8 h 0 min</td><td>8.00</td></tr>
      <tr><td>7:00 PM – 7:00 AM</td><td>45 min</td><td>11 h 15 min</td><td>11.25</td></tr>
      <tr><td>11:00 PM – 7:00 AM</td><td>none</td><td>8 h 0 min</td><td>8.00</td></tr>
      <tr><td>6:00 PM – 2:20 AM</td><td>20 min</td><td>8 h 0 min</td><td>8.00</td></tr>
      <tr><td>9:45 PM – 6:15 AM</td><td>30 min</td><td>8 h 0 min</td><td>8.00</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    A common mistake is subtracting the clock times directly: 6:30 − 10:00
    gives −3.5, and dropping the minus sign gives 3.5 hours instead of 8.5.
  </p>

  <h2>Which day do the hours belong to?</h2>
  <p>
    For a weekly total it rarely matters. But if you're paid overtime by the
    day, or your shift crosses the end of the workweek, it does.
  </p>
  <h3>The workweek boundary (US federal rule)</h3>
  <p>
    Under the Fair Labor Standards Act a workweek is a fixed, recurring
    period of 168 hours that can start on any day and at any hour, and each
    workweek stands alone. Hours are generally counted in the workweek in
    which they're worked. If your workweek starts at 12:00 AM Sunday, a
    Saturday 10:00 PM to Sunday 6:00 AM shift puts 2 hours in the week that
    is ending and 6 in the new one.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Example.</strong> You've worked 38 hours by Saturday evening and
    then work that 8-hour overnight shift. If the whole shift counted in the
    first week you'd have 46 hours and 6 hours of overtime. Split correctly,
    the first week has 40 hours and no overtime, and the 6 hours start the
    new week. That isn't your employer cheating; it's how the workweek
    works. But it is worth knowing where yours begins (ask payroll).
  </p>
  <h3>The workday (California and other daily-overtime rules)</h3>
  <p>
    California pays overtime after 8 hours in a <em>workday</em>, defined as
    any consecutive 24-hour period that starts at the same time each day. If
    your employer's workday starts at midnight, a 6:00 PM to 6:00 AM shift is
    6 hours in one workday and 6 in the next, so no daily overtime is owed
    for a 12-hour shift. If the workday starts at 6:00 PM, the same shift is
    12 hours in one workday: 4 hours of daily overtime. Employers are allowed
    to set the workday start, but it has to stay fixed. See
    <a href="/guides/california-overtime-rules/">California overtime rules</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    A shift that runs across two workdays is not a "split shift" under
    California law: the California Court of Appeal said so in
    <em>Securitas Security Services USA, Inc. v. Superior Court</em> (2011).
    Split shifts are covered in <a href="/guides/split-shift-pay/">split shift
    pay</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>When the clocks change overnight</h2>
  <p>
    On the nights daylight saving time starts or ends, the clock on the wall
    and the hours you actually work differ by one hour. Pay rules are based
    on the time actually worked:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Clocks go back (autumn):</strong> one hour happens twice (1:00 to 2:00 AM in North America and the UK). A 10:00 PM to 6:00 AM shift is <strong>9 hours</strong> of work, not 8.</li>
    <li><strong>Clocks go forward (spring):</strong> one hour is skipped (2:00 AM jumps to 3:00 AM in North America; 1:00 AM to 2:00 AM in the UK). A 10:00 PM to 6:00 AM shift is <strong>7 hours</strong> of work, not 8.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    US federal law requires pay for all hours worked, so on the autumn night
    the extra hour must be paid (and counts toward weekly overtime).
    Whether a salaried worker or someone on a fixed shift payment gets more
    or less pay in spring depends on the contract. Many time clocks record
    elapsed time correctly, but a handwritten timesheet or simple "end minus
    start" calculation, including ours, uses the clock on the wall. Add or
    subtract the hour yourself on these dates.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Region</th><th>Clocks go forward (−1 h)</th><th>Clocks go back (+1 h)</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>United States &amp; Canada (most areas)</td><td>Sun 8 Mar 2026 · Sun 14 Mar 2027</td><td>Sun 1 Nov 2026 · Sun 7 Nov 2027</td></tr>
      <tr><td>United Kingdom</td><td>Sun 29 Mar 2026 · Sun 28 Mar 2027</td><td>Sun 25 Oct 2026 · Sun 31 Oct 2027</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Australia (NSW, Victoria, ACT, Tasmania, SA)</td><td>Sun 4 Oct 2026 · Sun 3 Oct 2027</td><td>Sun 5 Apr 2026 · Sun 4 Apr 2027</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    The transitions happen in the early hours of each Sunday listed, so the
    affected shift is the one that starts on Saturday night. Arizona (except
    the Navajo Nation), Hawaii, most of Saskatchewan, and Queensland, Western
    Australia and the Northern Territory don't change their clocks. We
    worked out these dates from the IANA time zone database used by
    computers worldwide.
  </p>

  <h2>Breaks, rest and night pay</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Breaks</strong> follow the same rules as day shifts: in the US,
      short rest breaks are paid and genuine meal breaks of 30 minutes or more
      usually aren't. See <a href="/guides/are-breaks-paid/">are breaks paid?</a>
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Rest between shifts</strong> is protected in some places: 11 hours
      in the UK, and in Ontario 11 consecutive hours off each day and 8 hours
      between shifts (with exceptions). The US has no general federal rule.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Night premiums</strong> aren't required by US federal law, but
      many employers pay a shift differential. If yours does, it raises your
      overtime rate too. See <a href="/guides/shift-differential-pay/">shift
      differential pay</a>.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Calculate an overnight shift</h2>
  <p>
    The <a href="/calculators/hours-worked-calculator/">Hours Worked
    Calculator</a> spots an end time earlier than the start time and treats it
    as an overnight shift automatically. For a full week of night shifts with
    overtime and pay, use the
    <a href="/calculators/time-card-calculator/">Time Card Calculator</a>, which
    handles overnight shifts on every row.
  </p>

